---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const poems = await getCollection('poems');
const authors = await getCollection('authors');
---

<Base title="About" description="What this place is, and how it treats poems.">
  <h1>About this place</h1>
  <p>
    Withering Words is a quiet room for poems — {poems.length} of them so far,
    by {authors.length} poets. No accounts, no feeds, no comments. You can
    read <a href="/today/">today's poem</a>, follow a
    <a href="/moods/">mood</a>, <a href="/wander/">wander</a> at random, or
    <a href="/search/">search</a> for a line you half-remember.
  </p>
  <hr />
  <h2>The sound</h2>
  <p>
    Everything you can hear — the typewriter, the page turns, the birds, the
    stream, the rain — is synthesized in your browser while you listen.
    No recordings, nothing downloaded, nothing plays until you ask. The
    read-aloud voices are your own device's. It all lives behind the small
    ♪ in the corner.
  </p>
  <h2>The rights</h2>
  <p>
    Poets in the public domain appear in full. Poets still in copyright
    appear only in short excerpts — a few lines, always credited, always
    with a pointer to the book. Every poem's text is taken verbatim from a
    printed source, never from memory. Where a poem is translated, the
    translator is named; we use only translations old enough to belong to
    everyone.
  </p>
  <h2>The type</h2>
  <p>
    Set in EB Garamond on unbleached paper colors, warm by day and dim by
    night — following your system's preference, or the ☾ toggle in the
    header if you'd rather choose. Motion and sound respect your
    accessibility settings.
  </p>
  <p class="byline">Read slowly. Come back tomorrow.</p>
</Base>
